# Untitled

## Target-triggered hybridization chain reaction for ultrasensitive dual-signal miRNA detection
State Key Laboratory of Bioelectronics, National Demonstration Center for Experimental Biomedical Engineering Education, School of Biological Science and Medical Engineering, Southeast University, Nanjing, 210096, China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: DNA cascade Reaction Dual-signal readout Non-enzymatic Signal amplification MOFs derivative
A signal amplification sensing system with target-triggered DNA cascade reaction combined with dual-signal readout technology was designed for ultrasensitive analysis of miRNA. The highly conductive metal organic frameworks (MOFs) derivative, N-doped carbon dodecahedron (N-PCD) was deposited with gold nanoparticles as the electrode substrate, which could assist the electron transfer between the molecular probe and the electrode surface, and could remarkably enhance electrochemical response. Tetrahedral DNA nanostructure (T4-DNA) with high structural stability and mechanical stiffness was designed to improve the loading capacity and binding efficiency of the target, thus increasing the sensitivity of the system. The non-enzymatic amplification method based on the DNA cascade reaction allows the electrochemical responses from dual signal DNA probes labeled with ferrocene (Fc) and methylene blue (MB), respectively in turn to improve the reliability of detection. Under optimal conditions, the sensor has a linear range of \(5.1.0 \times 10^{4}\,\mathrm{fM}\), and the limit of detection is as low as \(1.92\,\mathrm{fM}\) and \(3.74\,\mathrm{fM}\) for Fc and MB labeled probe, respectively. This strategy raises the promising application for the rapid detection of miRNA targets with low abundance in complex biological systems.
## 1. Introduction
The abnormal expression of microRNAs (miRNAs) is closely related to a variety of human diseases, which can be stably present in serum and are considered as potential disease biomarkers (Mitchell et al., 2008; Chen et al., 2008). The miRNAs can act as tumor suppressors or oncogenes that participate in carcinogenesis (Peng and Croce, 2016). As one of the most abundant and highly conserved miRNAs known, miRNA-21 and miRNA-141 play an important role in carcinogenesis, and its high expression is associated with poor performance and shorter survival in patients (Pfeffer et al., 2015). In addition to evaluation of tumor efficacy, miRNA-21 is also associated with other diseases such as cardiovascular and inflammation. Davydova et al. found that miRNA-21 is associated with chronic heart failure and ischemic heart disease, with higher levels of miRNA-21 expression in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Davydova et al., 2020). However, miRNAs have high sequence similarity and low presence in tissues, blood, and cells.
miRNAs show ultra-low abundance \((<\!1.0\;\mathrm{pM})\) in normal human serum and fluctuate widely with disease progression (Xu et al., 2019). Yin et al. measured the concentration of microRNA-21 in normal hepatocytes to be \(0.127\,\mathrm{pM}\) (Yin et al., 2012). Zhang's group used electrochemical methods and quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction (qRT-PCR) to measure the concentration of microRNA-21 in the serum of five breast cancer patients to be 25.7–35.1 fM (Lu et al., 2019). Therefore, there is an urgent need for construction of a miRNAs analysis method suitable for early diagnosis of diseases.
In recent years, many analytical methods including qRT-PCR, microarray, electrochemistry, fluorescence, in situ hybridization, electrochemiluminescence, have been well developed for the detection of miRNAs (Chen et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2019). As one of the mainstream technologies, electrochemical biosensors are one of the bioanalytical platforms for rapid, simple, and sensitive detection of miRNAs. On this platform, numerous works have utilized the highly specific molecular recognition properties of DNA bases to design DNA nanostructures with
specific structures and superior functions, such as linear, stem-loop, triple-helix, tetrahedron, etc (Liedl et al., 2007; Qing et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2018; Ding et al., 2020; Huang et al., 2020; Li et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2018a, 2018b). Among them, the non-rigid DNA nanostructures with variable surface orientation were irregularly distributed on the electrode surface with cross-reaction and aggregation effects between probes, resulting in decreased sensitivity and stability. Due to the steric hindrance effect, the probe used to identify the target is partially hidden, reducing the loading and binding efficiency of the target, while the relatively rigid DNA nanostructures (such as triple helix, and tetrahedron) can greatly improve the detection accessibility and reduce non-specific adsorption. Sun's team achieved ultrasensitive detection of messenger RNA in serum extracellular vesicles through RNA extraction and enzymatic amplification based on DNA tetrahedral thermophoresis (Han et al., 2021).
For the detection of trace biomolecules in complex biological samples, sensitivity is an important parameter. Common signal amplification methods include enzyme-assisted amplification (Zhong et al., 2022), loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) (Zheng et al., 2021; Thi et al., 2020), rolling circle amplification (RCA) (Cui et al., 2017; Deng et al., 2014; Chen et al., 2022) and hybridization chain reaction (HCR) (Ren et al., 2018; Bi et al., 2017; Lu et al., 2020). HCR is an accelerated DNA cascade reaction based on the domino effect under enzyme-free conditions, which can form very long double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) and improve the detection signal of the target. The construction of the electrode interface is also a key factor to improve the stability and sensitivity of the sensor. As functionalized porous materials, zeolite imidazolate framework (ZIFs) are topological material of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) with tunable pore structures, and unique physicochemical properties, which are widely used in the construction of sensing interfaces (Mohan et al., 2022; Iacomi et al., 2022). Porous carbon materials can be obtained by direct high-temperature carbonization of MOFs materials. The MOFs derivatives have excellent properties and solve the problem of poor electrical conductivity of pure MOFs materials (Tang et al., 2015). Xu et al. (2017) reported the existence of graphitic carbon structures in ZIF-8 derivatives when the carbonization temperature reached \(1000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The highly graphitized structure with the porous structure can facilitate the transfer of electrons, thereby improving the sensitivity and selectivity of the detection platform.
In this paper, we designed a dual-signal readout system that utilizes DNA tetrahedral (T4-DNA) nanostructures to recognize targets and trigger DNA cascade reactions for efficient detection of miRNA-21 in cells and serum samples (Scheme 1). First, a high-temperature
carbonized ZIF-8 derivative, N-doped carbon dodecahedron (N-PCD) was coated on the electrode surface by a drop-coating method, and gold nanoparticles were electrochemically deposited as a novel interface probe. The T4-DNA containing thiol groups at three vertices was used as a capture probe to be immobilized on the electrode surface in a highly ordered manner through Au–S bonds, and the other vertex was used to identify the target on the electrode surface. N-PCD provides sufficient space for T4-DNA fixation and facilitates electron transfer. Subsequently, miRNA-21 initiates HCR through base-complementary pairing with Fc and MB-modified signaling probes (DNA1-Fc and DNA2-MB) to form long dsDNA amplification signals, with dual signal output (Fc and MB) to improve the accuracy of the sensor. At the same time, each signal is set as a control group to again verify the advantage of dual signal output. Using this sensor platform to detect multiple targets, MB and Fc are used as signal probes to detect miRNA-21 and miRNA-141, respectively. Still using MB as the detection signal of the miRNA-21. The target recognition sequence at the top of the tetrahedral DNA is changed to perform complementary base pairing with miRNA-141. miRNA-141 is quantified using the output signal of Fc (Figs. S3–8, details in Supporting Information). The sensor shows excellent sensitivity and accuracy for the detection of miRNA by non-enzyme catalyzed amplification technology combined with dual-signal readout system, and successfully realizes the determination of miRNA in cells and serum. This design may provide a useful platform for the efficient detection of trace nucleic acids in complex biological samples.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of ZIF-8 and N-PCD
ZIF-8 was prepared as follows (Xie et al., 2020; Kim et al., 2016): \(0.19\,\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}\) and \(0.29\,\mathrm{g}\) of 2-methylimidazole were dissolved completely in \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) and \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) methanol, respectively. Subsequently, 2-methylimidazole methanol solution was slowly poured into the Zn \((\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}\) solution. After stirring for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the color of the solution gradually changed from transparent to milky white, followed by standing for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). The product was collected after \(3\,\mathrm{min}\) centrifugation at \(10000\,\mathrm{rpm}\) and washed in absolute ethanol for 2–3 times, and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) under vacuum.
The N-PCD was synthesized as follows: the dried ZIF-8 was placed in a quartz boat as a template, and calcinated in a heating tube furnace by several treatment stages. In the first stage, the temperature of the tube furnace was raised from \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) within \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). Then the temperature was raised to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a speed of \(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) (the second stage).
In the third stage, the temperature was kept for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\), and then anneal to room temperature. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) was used to protect the material during the calcining process (Scheme 2).
## 2.2. Preparation of T4-DNA probe
The T4-DNA probe was prepared by a one-step method (Lin et al., 2019). Four single-stranded, precursor DNAs (a, b, c, d) were dissolved in \(10\,\mathrm{\mM}\) Tris-HCl buffer solution (containing \(20\,\mathrm{\mM}\) TCEP, \(50\,\mathrm{\mM}\) \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{pH}\,8.0\)) respectively, at a final concentration of \(4~\upmu\mathrm{M}\). All DNA solutions were mixed equally and placed in a polymerase chain reaction (PCR) instrument at \(95\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\), followed by annealing the mixture on ice at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and then store it at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.3. Fabrication of the biosensor
Firstly, the bare glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was polished, and then followed by dropping \(8\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) N-PCD solution \((1\,\mathrm{mg/mL})\). After drying, the above electrodes were placed in \(0.1\%\) \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) (containing \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KCl), and gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) were fixed on the electrode by electrodeposition to obtain AuNPs/N-PCD/GCE. T4-DNA solution \((8\,\upmu\mathrm{L}, 1\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was dropped onto the electrode to form T4-DNA/AuNPs/N-PCD/ GCE, and placed overnight at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Subsequently, 6-mercapto-1-hexanol (MCH) \((4\,\upmu\mathrm{L}, 1\,\mathrm{mM})\) was added and incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) to block the non-specific binding sites. Then \(8\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of miRNA-21 with different concentrations was applied to the electrode to complement and pair with the T4-DNA capture probe, and incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(70\,\mathrm{min}\). After washing with RNase-free water, the mixture of DNA1-Fc and DNA2-MB \((1\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was added to trigger the subsequent signal amplification reaction, and the HCR reaction was carried out at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(70\,\mathrm{min}\). Before each modification step, the electrodes were rinsed with TrisHCl buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\) to remove unbound material. As shown in Scheme 1, miRNA-21 can be quantitatively detected by dual signals of Fe and MB.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of ZIF-8 and N-PCD
The details of ZIF-8 and N-PCD were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Fig. 1A, the synthesized ZIF-8 shows uniform size with an average particle size of about \(600\,\mathrm{nm}\), with a smooth and angular surface. Slight agglomeration occurs in a small portion of the product, which may be caused by the high solubility of ZIF-8 in methanol solution (Fan et al., 2015). Fig. 1B shows that N-PCD still maintains the smooth surface of ZIF-8 without obvious collapse after calcination. The TEM images of N-PCD (Fig. 1C and D) demonstrate the interior of N-PCD becomes hollow and the edge is irregular, which is conducive to the deposition of AuNPs.
becomes hollow and the edge is irregular, which is conducive to the deposition of AuNPs.
The crystal structure of the synthesized ZIF-8 and N-PCD is characterized by X-ray diffractometer (XRD) and Raman spectra. Fig. 2A and B, the diffraction peaks of ZIF-8 locate at \(7.1^{\circ}\), \(10.6^{\circ}\), \(12.5^{\circ}\), \(14.6^{\circ}\), \(16.7^{\circ}\), \(18.3^{\circ}\), \(22.3^{\circ}\), \(24.1^{\circ}\), \(25.7^{\circ}\), \(26.6^{\circ}\), \(29.5^{\circ}\), \(30.3^{\circ}\), \(31.2^{\circ}\), and \(31.9^{\circ}\), corresponding to diffraction crystal planes of (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), (222), (114), (233), (224), (134), (044), (334), (244) and (235), respectively, which is consistent with the reports (Li et al., 2019). There are no other impurity peaks in the XRD pattern, indicating the high purity of the samples. After calcination, the XRD pattern of N-PCD only shows two broad peaks at \(25.35^{\circ}\) and \(44.67^{\circ}\), corresponding to (002) and (101) crystal planes, respectively. The characteristic diffraction peak at \(25.35^{\circ}\) indicates a graphene-like structure in the derivative of ZIF-8.
Fig. 2C and D shows the Raman spectra of ZIF-8 and N-PCD, respectively. ZIF-8 exhibits typical vibration modes, such as strong \(\mathrm{Zn-N}\) stretching \((165\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\), imidazole ring folding \((687\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\), C5–N stretching \((1144\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) and methyl bending \((1460\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\). There are three \(\mathrm{C-H}\) out-of-plane bends in the range of \(800{-}1100\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The \(\mathrm{C-H}\) out-of-plane bending over methyl is observed at \(2932\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (Kumari et al., 2013). As to N-PCD (Fig. 2D), the two characteristic diffraction peaks at \(1320.60\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1583.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to peak D and peak G, respectively (Xie et al., 2020). The peak D is related to defects and disordered carbon in N-PCD, and the peak G is related to graphitic carbon in N-PCD, with an \(\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}\) of \(0.83\).
## 3.2. T4-DNA characterization
The synthesized T4-DNA was characterized by \(3.5\%\) agarose gel electrophoresis and atomic force microscopy (AFM). As shown in Fig. 3A, the four single strands (a, b, c and d) that make up T4-DNA are shown in lanes 2–5. The T4-DNA in lane 6 has a three-dimensional structure, whose migration rate is much lower than that of other lanes, indicating the successful preparation of T4-DNA. The AFM image in Fig. 3B also shows the synthesized T4-DNA appears as triangular particles of uniform size. Under strong electrostatic force and dehydration, T4-DNA with a three-dimensional structure may collapse into a two-dimensional triangular structure, which is consistent with the results reported in the literature (Lin et al., 2019; He et al., 2008). The above results indicated the successful preparation of T4-DNA.
## 3.3. Electrochemical study on signal amplification by N-PCD
The electron transfer processes on N-PCD modified GCE were investigated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) at different scan rates. As shown in Fig. 4A, as the scan rate increases, the peak potential changes
constantly. \( E_{pa} \) shifts from \( 0.250\:\mathrm{V} \) to positive potential to \( 0.273\:\mathrm{V} \), and \( E_{pc} \) slightly shifts from \( 0.179\:\mathrm{V} \) to negative potential to \( 0.153\:\mathrm{V} \), and \( I_{pa}/I_{pc} \) is about 1, indicating that the oxidation-reduction reaction of N-PCD/ GCE is a quasi-reversible process. The inset of Fig. 4A shows an excellent
linear relationship between \( I_{pa} \) and \( I_{pc} \) and the square root of the scanning speed \( (\nu^{1/2}) \), which accords with the characteristics of a quasi-reversible reaction. The regression equation: \( I_{pa} \left( \mu \mathrm{A} \right) = 2.43 + 15.44 \nu^{1/2} \) and \( I_{pc} \left( \mu \mathrm{A} \right) = 0.68 - 16.15 \nu^{1/2} \), and both correlation coefficients are
0.999. Therefore, the redox reaction and electron transfer of N-PCD/ GCE on the electrode surface are mainly based on diffusion-controlled process.
The chronocoulometric method is used to detect the effective specific surface area of GCE and AuNPs/N-PCD/GCE. Fig. 4B and its inset show Q-t curve and \(\mathsf{Q}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{t}^{1/2}\) curve, respectively. There is a good linear relationship between Q and \(\mathbf{t}^{1/2}\). According to formula: \(\mathrm{Q}=2\mathrm{nFAcD}^{1/2}\mathrm{t}^{1/2}/\pi^{1/2}+\mathrm{Q_{dl}}+\mathrm{Q_{ads}}\), where A \((\mathsf{c m}^{2})\) is effective specific surface area of the electrode surface, c \((\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{cm}^{3})\) is the concentration of substrate, \(\mathrm{Q_{dl}}\) (C)
and \(\mathrm{Q_{ads}}\) (C) is the double-layer charge and the adsorption charge, respectively (Sun et al., 2021). For \(0.1\;\mathrm{mM}\) \([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{3-}\), n is 1 and D is \(7.6\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{s}\). The calculated effective specific surface area of GCE and AuNPs/N-PCD/GCE are \(0.059\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) and \(0.17\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). The results demonstrate that AuNPs and N-PCD could provide more active sites to combine biomolecules, to further improve the sensitivity of the sensor. As shown in Fig. 4C and D, the differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) in the presence and absence of N-PCD was carried out respectively. Compared with the electrode without N-PCD, the DPV response signal
after N-PCD immobilized on the electrode surface increased by \(147.4\%\) and \(200.5\%\), respectively, thus further confirming the signal amplification effect by N-PCD.
The step-by-step electrode modification was also characterized by CV and EIS. Fig. 5A and B shows CV diagrams of electrodes modified with different materials. The peak current of GCE (curve a) modified with N-PCD increases significantly (curve b), indicating N-PCD has good electrical conductivity and can improve the electron transferability. With AuNPs electrodeposited on N-PCD/GCE (curve c), the peak current further increases by \(10\%\), suggesting AuNPs possess excellent conductivity, which can further increase the electron transfer rate. Note that N-PCD and AuNPs have a synergistic effect, which improves the conductivity of the electrode surface and the sensitivity of the sensor. Since then, with each additional step of modification involving DNA/RNA on the electrode, a decrease in \(I_{pa}\) and \(I_{pc}\) occurs. After the immobilization of capture probe, T4-DNA on the surface of the electrode through the Au–S bond (curve d), its negative charge and large steric hindrance reduce the electron transfer efficiency. Subsequently, the added MCH can block non-specific sites, which further reduces the peak current (curve e). Subsequently, after incubation of the target miRNA and DNA1-Fc and DNA2-MB labeled with electroactive molecules (curve f and g), the redox peak current further reduces, which is mainly related to the increase of the negative charge on the electrode surface and the increase of steric hindrance. Generally, after different modification, the changes in the redox peak currents are consistent with the expectation, proving the successful construction of electrochemical biosensors.
As a counterpart, Fig. 5C and D shows the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) diagrams of electrodes after previous modification steps. The inset in Fig. 4C shows the corresponding equivalent circuit diagram. Compared with \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) of \(250\ \Omega\) on GCE (curve a), the graphitic carbon structure containing N-PCD, can accelerate the electron transfer rate on the electrode surface, and the \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) value reduces to \(130\,\Omega\) (curve b). The electrodeposition of AuNPs on N-PCD further boosts the charge transfer rate, which further reduces the \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) value (curve c). After the modification of T4-DNA, the negative charge on the electrode surface increases and the steric hindrance increases, and the \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) values continue to increase (curve c-g). The EIS experimental results are
consistent with the CV results, which further proves the successful construction of the sensor.
## 3.4. Analytical performance of biosensors and real sample analysis
In order to obtain the optimal performance of the constructed sensor, the deposition time of Au, the incubation time of miRNA, and the concentration and the incubation time of the electroactive markers DNA1-Fc and DNA2-MB were optimized (Fig. S1, details in Supporting Information).
Under optimized experimental conditions, the relationship between DPV peak currents and miRNA-21 with different concentrations. As shown in Fig. 6A and B, the peak currents of signal probes Fc and MB gradually increased with the increase of miRNA-21 concentration from 0 to 10000 fM. The inset in Fig. 6B shows an excellent linear relationship between the peak currents of probes Fc and MB and the logarithm of miRNA-21 concentration, which can be expressed as \(I_{\mathrm{Fc}}\,(\upmu\mathrm{A})=1.55\log(c)+23.57\) and \(I_{\mathrm{MB}}\,(\upmu\mathrm{A})=1.44\log(c)+21.40\) with a correlation coefficient of 0.998 and 0.996, respectively. The limits of detection (LOD) are 1.92 fM for Fc and 3.74 fM for MB \((\mathrm{S/N}=3)\). The comparison of the electrochemical performance of this work with other methods is summarized in Table S2, which indicates the fabricated biosensor shows a wider linear range and a lower LOD. In Table S3, ZIF-8 derivative-modified biosensors have better performance than the miRNA sensors constructed from other materials. More importantly, compared with single-signal systems, the detection results of dual-signal readout systems are more accurate.
To investigate the selectivity of the sensor, different RNA sequences with concentrations of 2000 fM were chosen as interferents, including miRNA-21, miRNA-141, miRNA-155, single-based mismatch RNA (smRNA), three-based mismatch RNA (tmRNA), non-complementary RNA (NC RNA), base mutant RNA (BM RNA) and base deletion mutant RNA (BDM RNA). As shown in Fig. 6C, the peak currents in the presence of other interfering miRNAs have no significant difference from those in the absence of the target, indicating that the sensor has excellent selectivity for miRNA-21.
In addition, to evaluate the reproducibility of the sensor, the DPV
signal was measured on six electrodes modified under same conditions. The relative standard deviation (RSD) was \(4.9\%\) for the six electrodes, demonstrating the sensor showed excellent reproducibility. To further verify the stability of the sensor, five electrodes prepared in parallel were selected, stored in a \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 10 days, and tested every 2 days. As shown in Fig. S2A, the response signals of Fc and MB basically have no change, which remains at about \(98.7\%\) and \(97.4\%\), illustrating the prepared sensor display a long-term stability.
To verify the applicability of the sensor in cell samples, miRNA-21 was detected in L02 and MCF-7 cells. Fig. S2B shows the electropherogram of total RNA extracted from different cells (details in Supporting Information). The constructed electrochemical sensor was used to detect miRNA-21 in total RNA. As shown in Fig. 6D, the peak current of dual signals in MCF-7 cells was significantly higher than that in L02 cells, demonstrating the expression level of miRNA-21 in MCF-7 was higher than that in L02. This is consistent with the experimental results of upregulation of miRNA-21 expression in breast cancer reported in literature (Wickramasinghe et al., 2009).
In order to further demonstrate the performance of the constructed electrochemical biosensor in real sample, the recovery experiment was carried out. Samples 1 and 2 were from healthy subjects, and samples 3 and 4 were from breast cancer patients. All serum samples were centrifuged 3 times at \(8000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(3\ \mathrm{min}\). After centrifugation, the serum samples were diluted by 10 times with \(10~\mathrm{mM}\) PBS buffer (pH 7.4). As shown in Table S4, only spiking miRNA-21 can be accurately detected in sample 1 and 2, while endogenous miRNA-21 was also found in samples 3 and 4. The recoveries of miRNA-21 in all samples ranged from \(92.16\%\) to \(106.57\%\), proving this method can be used for the detection of miRNA-21 in real samples.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, an electrochemical biosensor based on non-enzyme catalyzed amplification technology and dual-signal readout technology was constructed to detect miRNA in cells and serum with high accuracy and sensitivity. This method integrates multiple signal amplification techniques, with typical advantages: (i) Au-deposited N-PCD acts as a scaffold with a large binding area and high conductivity for easy
inmobilization of a large amount of T4-DNA on the electrode; (ii) T4-DNA acts as a rigid scaffold to improve target accessibility and loading; (iii) The domino effect was used to accelerate the HCR cascade and to increase the concentration of signaling probes. (iv) Dual signals from independent DNA probes help to obtain more accurate and reliable detection results. Therefore, the proposed sensor shows excellent detection performances and great potential in complex biological samples and clinical analysis.
